Here is the essay rewritten into English:

---

Three hours ago, I got off work and went home, went online, and saw people mourning #Yu Yingshi. At the time, all I knew was that he was the idol of the famous blogger Professor #Wang Weixiong; other than that, I knew nothing about his life.

But in the 21st century, you don't need to ask #teachers for everything—with Google, you can know everything under the sun. After searching for a while, I increasingly understood why he was so famous, and why Professor Wang loved to bring up Old Yu...

Old Yu was a professor at three Ivy League schools! How impressive is that! Just dropping his name is enough to scare people to death!

I was like a stalker, constantly digging up Yu Yingshi's background information: how Chinese Wikipedia wrote about him, how English Wikipedia wrote about him, how people praised him when he won awards, and even how today's obituaries talk about him. In my mind, the person I respect most emerged... Teacher Yungen. They really are quite similar!

Unfortunately, Teacher Gen doesn't have a Harvard professorship, but their traditional Chinese kung fu of #knowing the past and present, #I am China, is definitely from the same school. Teacher Gen likes Daoism and Buddhism, while Old Yu seems to be a Confucian fan.

But that's where it gets tragic.

Confucianism emphasizes benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and trustworthiness; order between elders and juniors; rulers acting as rulers and ministers as ministers. But if you're studying at a top American university, just getting your PhD in the era when hippies were most prevalent, are you really going to go around teaching people to #respect teachers and value their teachings, and not to engage in so much #individualism? That would leave you begging on the streets.

Speaking of which, how scholars avoid begging on the streets is a lesson Confucius learned through a lifetime of hardship—Confucian disciples couldn't possibly not know it: In Confucius's era, great chaos was about to descend upon the world. He traveled through the states, hoping to secure an official position and accomplish great things. But all he knew was reciting books and talking about the rituals of the Duke of Zhou; he didn't know how to cater to his boss's needs, and ultimately returned empty-handed. However, failure is the mother of success—his students improved greatly. The Grand Historian said: "The seventy disciples scattered among the feudal lords; the greatest became teachers and prime ministers, the lesser befriended and taught scholars and officials." Why?

Because after several generations of transmission, they began to learn how to read the monarch's expressions. By Mencius's time, his interactions with rulers had become somewhat different—joking and laughing, knowing how to please the boss: "King of Qi, I can see you can't bear to kill even an ox, so you must be a benevolent ruler! Practice the kingly way—you can do it!"

By the end of the Warring States period, those "Confucian" strategists had perfected the game of "monarch interviews." In order to become officials, they abandoned "benevolence" and "propriety" altogether, and all went to study "rule of law," this new technology. So Xunzi, as a Confucian disciple, taught students who could become high officials in Qin Shi Huang's government—from Li Si, you certainly can't smell even a trace of Confucius's spirit.

Today, the UN has over a hundred and fifty countries, and the situation is more complex than the Spring and Autumn period. Confucian heirs leave China, this (ancient) land of propriety, and go to the barbaric land of the military hegemon, America—are they really going to pull out the *Analects* and tell people to #observe three years of mourning? Everyone knows well how humiliating it was when Confucius and his students were turned away at every door. So, upon arriving in America, Confucian disciples naturally say that the Master supported #democracy, #democracy is great, and the Communist Party is damned to hell—that's how you get an Ivy League professorship!

Someone might ask: Doesn't talking nonsense have consequences? Of course not! Confucius and Mencius died over two thousand years ago, and the Confucian tradition was smashed to pieces during the #May Fourth Movement. As an academic authority at a top university in the only superpower after World War II, you could say that Johnny Kung taught people to do drugs and sleep around and people might still believe you—after all, America's #theoretical nuclear bombs are the real deal. The consequences of the Japanese not believing it are still fresh in everyone's memory.

So why could Old Yu casually translate a few passages from the *Book of Han* into English and get a PhD, and have several universities hire him as a professor? #You know why. They push you up onto the altar as an authority precisely so you'll say, with conviction: "Everyone here is a dead duck. You think Confucian thought is feudal? Oh, what a misunderstanding! In fact, he was a fighter for democracy! If you don't have a Harvard professorship, just listen quietly!"

As for whether Confucianism conflicts with democracy? Honestly, what would "Sinologists" know about that? You say: The Republic of China is an example. Hey, buddy, is Taiwan's political system really "Confucian"? The Kuomintang bastards curse Tsai Ing-wen every day—is that how Confucianism works? Does Tsai Ing-wen put on a show of worshipping heaven and earth? No, she doesn't. Scientific spirit, not being a charlatan—that goes against Confucian principles. You think using Chinese characters makes you Confucian? You think Confucianism can only exist in China?

As I said earlier, Japan is Confucian, and Britain is Confucian (at least among the world's advanced countries, they're the closest to Confucian): the ruler emphasizes ritual and pomp, power is bestowed by heaven, supreme, not to be cursed; everyone is in layers of hierarchy, no challenging your superiors, and only then is society stable. As for the successes and failures of their democratic systems, that's not something a Chinese-American has the qualifications to comment on.

---

**Commentary:**

Disregarding the sarcastic tone, the author's main point is that Yu Yingshi's academic career and the Western (particularly American) Sinological establishment's promotion of him as a Confucian authority were not purely scholarly achievements, but rather politically motivated. The author argues that:

1. Western academia elevated Yu Yingshi (and similar scholars) to authoritative status not because of genuine intellectual merit, but because his interpretation of Confucianism—framing it as compatible with democracy and critical of the Chinese Communist Party—served Western ideological interests during the Cold War and after.

2. The author claims that real Confucianism, as practiced historically, was hierarchical, ritualistic, and authoritarian (citing examples like Japan and Britain as "true" Confucian societies), and that the modern reinterpretation of Confucius as a "democratic fighter" is a distortion created to serve political purposes.

3. The essay suggests that scholars like Yu Yingshi, by presenting Confucianism as proto-democratic, provided intellectual cover for Western hegemony while delegitimizing the Chinese political system.

In essence, the author is critiquing the instrumentalization of Confucian scholarship for Cold War-era ideological purposes, arguing that the "Confucian democracy" narrative is a Western construct imposed on a tradition that was actually hierarchical and authoritarian. [end of text]


